00:00:18.774 --> 00:00:20.190
You might be asking
yourself, what

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.020
makes handling streaming
data different?

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.520
Well, when you've
got lots of data--

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.179
and I mean lots of data--
constantly coming in,

00:00:26.179 --> 00:00:28.470
you can't just process it
the same way as a large batch

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:29.142
dataset.

00:00:29.142 --> 00:00:31.350
We need to make sure we
handle the data appropriately

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:32.610
as it comes in.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.160
Streaming analysis tools let
us work with unbounded data

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.570
and handle challenges
such as unknown delays.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.870
This is especially common
for mobile users and devices

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.262
that aren't always connected.

00:00:41.262 --> 00:00:42.720
Late data will
still need to factor

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.520
into your data processing.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.020
In addition, we
often want to work

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.811
with grouping our data
into meaningful sets

00:00:47.811 --> 00:00:49.390
by factors like time.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:50.540
This is called windowing.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.130
And we can do this by dividing
our events into fixed time

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.440
intervals, sliding
time intervals,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.680
or based on when a user is
active and sending events.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.390
So knowing some of
the challenges that

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.642
make streaming analysis unique,
let's implement an example.

00:01:03.642 --> 00:01:05.850
If we were going to build
a game with lots of events,

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.250
we can use the Google Cloud
Dataflow and the Apache Beam

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.702
SDK to do this.

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:11.910
We can implement data
processing with event handling,

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.556
and we'll build a pipeline
using some common patterns.

00:01:14.556 --> 00:01:15.930
First, we'll want
to make sure we

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.180
handle each event
only once through

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.710
exactly-once processing.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.070
We can use Google Cloud PubSub
to handle all of the events,

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.170
but we'll add in some
deduplication logic

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.651
right in Dataflow to avoid
handling the same event twice.

00:01:28.651 --> 00:01:30.150
Next, we'll want
to make sure we can

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.770
handle bad data that comes in.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.690
By default, Dataflow
will continuously

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.704
retry those records, because
we don't want to lose data.

00:01:36.704 --> 00:01:38.370
Instead of that, let's
build in a system

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.080
to handle malformed
data, storing it

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.910
in BigQuery for
further analysis,

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.100
and setting up alerts in
Google Cloud Functions.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.440
Now we know that
data won't be lost.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.270
Even when we protect
against bad data,

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.130
sometimes additional
data comes in,

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.080
but we actually still
want to capture it.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.320
Thankfully, BigQuery allows
for additive schema changes.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.880
If additional data
comes in that goes

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.620
beyond our original
schema, we can

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.960
have BigQuery update the
fields without any downtime

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.520
and reinsert it.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.060
Of course, with
all of that data,

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.250
there are other parts
of our architecture

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.333
that might have important
information for our game

00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:08.070
events.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:11.334
In order to enrich our data with
some real-time denormalization,

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:12.750
we'll add a step
for grabbing data

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.797
from another source,
such as Bigtable.

00:02:14.797 --> 00:02:16.380
During the data flow
processing, we'll

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.352
make a connection to
Bigtable, pull out

00:02:18.352 --> 00:02:20.310
the information we need,
and then add that back

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:21.330
into our pipeline.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:24.777
Finally, let's do some user
behavior analysis on our data.

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:26.610
Now that we've got all
of our enriched data,

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.920
we want to look at windowing
our data with sessions so

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.230
that we know when the
user is playing the game.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.570
Also, grouping by
user ID lets us fully

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.540
analyze what events a user
generates during an activity

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.770
session.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.110
Now that is a streaming
analytics solution.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.905
In the video, Ryan
gives more detail

00:02:41.905 --> 00:02:44.280
about how you can implement
these, as well as a great use

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.890
case from Sky, a television
provider in Europe that

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.690
saw some fantastic
benefits when switching

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.852
from an on-prem solution
to using Dataflow and Beam.

